import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as r,q as n}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var a=i("<h1>The Hearthstone Protocol v2.0.3</h1> <h2>Voluntary Commons Conversion, Rematriation, Stewardship Trusts, and Constitutionally Bounded Asset Transition</h2> <p><strong>Framework ID:</strong> <code>framework_property_stewardship</code><br> <strong>Version:</strong> 2.0.3<br> <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-08<br> <strong>Tier:</strong> 2<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Controlled constitutional successor — person-continuity conformance patch <strong>Implementation priority:</strong> Critical<br> <strong>Conforms to:</strong> <code>CERGTA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Person-continuity interface:</strong> Constitutional Interface Specification <code>CIS/0.2.4</code><br> <strong>Data/identity boundary:</strong> Aurora Accord v1.3.2<br> <strong>Supersedes:</strong> Hearthstone Protocol v1.5<br> <strong>Authority effect:</strong> None until lawfully adopted in a jurisdiction</p> <hr> <h2>0. Controlled successor notice</h2> <p>This document preserves the Hearthstone vision of moving from extractive ownership toward shared, regenerative stewardship while replacing the v1.5 assumption that one framework can move directly from moral purpose, ecological metrics, spiritual recognition, or political commitment to title transfer, stewardship revocation, tariffs, arrest warrants, emergency receivership, or external enforcement.</p> <p>Hearthstone v2.0 is a <strong>transition architecture</strong>, not a global property sovereign, court, registry sovereign, police service, taxing authority, emergency command, or universal representative of Indigenous peoples.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Commons purpose does not create commons jurisdiction.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>Voluntary conversion is not rematriation. Rematriation is not confiscation. Rights recognition is not title. Sacredness is not conveyance. Stewardship is not ownership. Performance evidence is not revocation. Urgency is not receivership. A registry is not law.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>The protocol may invite, advise, fund where separately authorized, mediate, provide model instruments, support claims, facilitate voluntary transactions, prepare lawful records, monitor agreed stewardship duties, and route disputes. It may not compel transfer, extinguish claims, appoint guardians, designate sacredness for another people, issue tariffs, freeze funds, impose sanctions, arrest, seize, restrain, or use force.</p> <p><strong>v2.0.2 labour-boundary clarification.</strong> Hearthstone owns the worker, contractor, pension, livelihood, and service-continuity consequences of an asset or stewardship transition. It does not create ordinary employment law. Substantive wages, employment contracts, bargaining rights, union recognition, dismissal, severance, occupational safety, working time, pensions, unemployment entitlements, and worker classification remain with competent labour/employment law, collective agreements, contracts, regulators, tribunals, and other valid instruments. <code>HSP-21</code> makes those interests visible during transition; it does not constitute them.</p> <p><strong>v2.0.3 person-continuity clarification.</strong> Hearthstone may identify claimants and stewards, correct registry collisions, and consume externally constituted status events where required for an asset/stewardship transition. It does not create general civil identity, register death, determine heirs, or turn its registry into a universal person graph. CIS/0.2.4 governs purpose-limited same-person continuity; Aurora v1.3.2 governs anti-linkage and data-purpose constraints. Pairwise continuity is non-transitive by default, and succession questions remain with competent succession/property/family/Indigenous/customary law.</p> <h2>1. Source freeze and controlling instruments</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hearthstone Protocol v1.5</td><td>v1.5(2).md</td><td><code>5be6371227630557ea481813ae9073ec0f5dc8d32477c3342ceb7f506002cbbd</code></td></tr><tr><td>Commons/Ecological Authority Audit v0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-constitutional-audit-v0.1.md</td><td><code>974dfd8dd4bf5b623bd5e7e544b7afdbbd89869b66f107db293cfe6af67a043b</code></td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>3c45e37c834c727471657fc91149edbe3011137562c2dd73e54b20226c92589b</code></td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7</td><td>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.md</td><td><code>41cb7e607e8c3f8776ca74f3ba46ab0ff6bcce94fc24b35178fc4cd94d80d9a2</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planetary Health v0.9</td><td>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.md</td><td><code>259083f6039180b4532ae168ff441245e526570c6442371814cbb238e669dc13</code></td></tr><tr><td>SCPA/0.1</td><td>security-coercion-protective-action-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>b8f1dd0f676044542a23184f52200e50b106a798545c7c57119ded2c439b6299</code></td></tr><tr><td>ECRC/0.1</td><td>emergency-catastrophic-risk-continuity-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>c673742b1317c7169d4b8226118478c990e9a3d05084b2dc5ac9ad2f1971163c</code></td></tr></tbody></table> <p>Version substitution is not automatic. A future version must pass an explicit compatibility review before it may replace a pinned instrument in a consequential case.</p> <p>This table is a <strong>provenance record</strong>: it identifies the exact texts read and audited when this successor was drafted. It is not the operative dependency set. The instruments this patch interoperates with are listed in Appendix A.1, and the authoritative machine-readable set is the cluster registry named in A.4. Where the two differ, A.1 governs. Digests are content hashes and remain valid under renaming; a filename here identifies an instrument, not a storage location.</p> <h2>2. Vision</h2> <p>To provide legal, ethical, financial, cultural, and practical pathways by which land, enterprises, infrastructure, capital, cultural assets, and other shared resources may move—voluntarily or through separately lawful public, reparative, or adjudicative processes—from extractive control toward regenerative stewardship.</p> <p>The protocol seeks a commonwealth in which:</p> <ul><li>ecological and cultural relationships receive meaningful legal protection;</li> <li>Indigenous nations may pursue Land Back, rematriation, restitution, and reparations through their own authority and applicable law;</li> <li>workers, residents, tenants, users, and communities participate in governance without losing existing rights;</li> <li>owners and enterprises may choose respected, economically viable stewardship transitions;</li> <li>claims are heard before interests are extinguished;</li> <li>guardians represent without owning;</li> <li>stewards exercise bounded mandates rather than absolute title power;</li> <li>essential services survive every transition;</li> <li>metrics inform learning without becoming automated government;</li> <li>every exceptional power expires and every authority returns.</li></ul> <h2>3. Scope</h2> <p>Hearthstone applies to proposed transitions involving:</p> <ul><li>land and buildings;</li> <li>farms, forests, watersheds, sacred and cultural places;</li> <li>enterprises, cooperatives, and productive infrastructure;</li> <li>housing, community facilities, and urban commons;</li> <li>shared capital, endowments, funds, and debt arrangements;</li> <li>public, private, customary, collective, trust, leasehold, and mixed forms;</li> <li>cultural, knowledge, data, and digital commons only where the competent cultural, privacy, data, and Indigenous authorities permit it.</li></ul> <p>Hearthstone does not itself govern ecological rights recognition, compulsory acquisition, taxation, criminal liability, trade sanctions, border action, search, seizure, custody, force, or emergency command.</p> <h2>4. Foundational principles</h2> <ol><li><strong>Right relationship without jurisdictional fiction.</strong> Stewardship expresses relationship and duty, but relationship alone does not create legal power.</li> <li><strong>Affected-nation authority.</strong> The specific Indigenous nation or nations affected determine their own representation, consent, law, sacred relations, knowledge, and preferred remedy.</li> <li><strong>Claims before conveyance.</strong> Title, occupancy, use, tenancy, labour, customary, public, and reparative claims must be mapped before consequential transfer.</li> <li><strong>Stewardship over absolutism.</strong> Stewardship entities receive enumerated purposes and duties rather than undefined sovereignty.</li> <li><strong>Voluntary means voluntary.</strong> A voluntary pathway must be genuinely withdrawable until the legally defined commitment point and may not hide penalties for refusal.</li> <li><strong>Public and reparative law remain separate.</strong> Expropriation, restitution, reparations, rematriation orders, and public acquisition require their own lawful basis and process.</li> <li><strong>No deprivation through transition.</strong> Essential water, sanitation, housing, healthcare, food, disability support, energy, wages, pensions, and emergency access survive conversion disputes.</li> <li><strong>Metric humility.</strong> BHI, LMCI, Love Ledger, Proof of Care, dashboards, citizen observations, and AI outputs inform review but do not create title or consequence.</li> <li><strong>Just transition before displacement.</strong> Workers, residents, tenants, users, creditors, and dependent communities receive continuity and remedy before structural change is completed.</li> <li><strong>Subsidiarity with legal clarity.</strong> Decisions should be made at the lowest capable and legitimate level, but local proximity does not substitute for law, affected-nation authority, or rights.</li> <li><strong>Guardianship is fiduciary representation.</strong> A guardian does not own the ecosystem, land, or trust and does not acquire general political or enforcement power.</li> <li><strong>Exit is constitutional.</strong> Stewardship arrangements must provide lawful amendment, succession, withdrawal, dissolution, and title destination.</li> <li><strong>Every authority ends.</strong> Receivership, transition offices, interim restrictions, credentials, data access, and delegated powers expire or return.</li></ol> <h2>5. Legal and adoption states</h2> <p>Every Hearthstone instrument must identify one of these states:</p> <ul><li><code>conceptual</code>;</li> <li><code>advisory</code>;</li> <li><code>offered_for_voluntary_adoption</code>;</li> <li><code>contractually_accepted</code>;</li> <li><code>enacted_in_local_law</code>;</li> <li><code>treaty_active_in_named_jurisdiction</code>;</li> <li><code>judicially_ordered</code>;</li> <li><code>suspended</code>;</li> <li><code>expired_or_terminated</code>.</li></ul> <p>A target date, framework publication, BAZ vote, council endorsement, pilot milestone, treaty aspiration, percentage of converted assets, or dashboard status does not change the adoption state by itself.</p> <h2>6. Institutional architecture</h2> <h3>6.1 Commons Transition Support Network</h3> <p>The <strong>Commons Transition Support Network (CTSN)</strong> is a non-sovereign service network. It may maintain:</p> <ul><li>model contracts and trust instruments;</li> <li>claimant and affected-party checklists;</li> <li>transition-finance templates;</li> <li>legal-aid and mediation rosters;</li> <li>worker, tenant, user, and service-continuity tools;</li> <li>governance pattern libraries;</li> <li>accessibility and offline-record systems;</li> <li>training, research, and implementation learning;</li> <li>compatibility registers for CERGTA and related interfaces.</li></ul> <p>It may not:</p> <ul><li>declare property defective;</li> <li>compel participation;</li> <li>certify the true Indigenous representative for another people;</li> <li>designate sacredness;</li> <li>transfer title;</li> <li>adjudicate competing claims;</li> <li>appoint ecological guardians;</li> <li>impose tax, tariff, sanction, freeze, receivership, or market exclusion;</li> <li>command law enforcement or emergency response.</li></ul> <h3>6.2 Local Commons Transition Offices</h3> <p>A participating jurisdiction or affected nation may establish a local office under its own law. Its functions may include legal information, counselling, facilitation, valuation procurement, document preparation, capacity support, public education, and case coordination.</p> <p>Its legal identity, appointment, budget, conflicts, review, expiry, and relationship to courts, registries, Indigenous authorities, and public agencies must be recorded in <code>HSP-01</code>.</p> <h3>6.3 Competent external authorities</h3> <p>The following remain outside Hearthstone unless a jurisdiction lawfully assigns them to a named institution:</p> <ul><li>land and company registries;</li> <li>courts and tribunals;</li> <li>tax and public-finance authorities;</li> <li>expropriation, restitution, and reparations authorities;</li> <li>affected Indigenous governments and institutions;</li> <li>permit and resource-allocation authorities;</li> <li>inspection and investigation bodies;</li> <li>emergency authorities;</li> <li>enforcement services.</li></ul> <p>Referral is not transfer of power. Advice is not acceptance. Coordination is not joint command.</p> <h2>7. Transition pathways</h2> <p>Hearthstone recognizes distinct pathways that may be combined only when each retains separate authority, consent, records, clocks, and remedies.</p> <h3>7.1 Voluntary donation or dedication</h3> <p>An owner or competent collective may donate or dedicate an asset subject to title verification, informed authorization, creditor and tax treatment, affected-party continuity, and registrable conveyance.</p> <h3>7.2 Voluntary sale or public-interest purchase</h3> <p>A community, nation, trust, fund, or public authority may purchase an asset through transparent valuation and finance. Ecological or sacred significance may justify fundraising priority; it does not erase bargaining rights or compensation rules.</p> <h3>7.3 Cooperative or steward-ownership conversion</h3> <p>An enterprise may become a worker cooperative, multi-stakeholder cooperative, purpose trust, foundation-owned company, stewardship trust, or another recognized form. Employee votes, fiduciary duties, minority rights, pensions, creditors, tax, and governance must follow applicable law.</p> <h3>7.4 Inheritance and bequest</h3> <p>A person may bequeath assets to an eligible stewardship entity subject to succession law, family and dependent protections, creditor rights, tax, capacity, and challenge.</p> <p>Hearthstone may record that a competent external authority has established death or another succession-relevant status event and may apply only the transition consequences within its lawful mandate. It does not register the death, determine heirs, probate validity, family status, legal capacity, or inheritance priority. Inactivity, registry silence, non-use of an asset, disappearance, or non-response does not establish death.</p> <p>Where person continuity across claimant, owner, beneficiary, trustee, steward, or successor records is consequential, the relation follows CIS/0.2.4: purpose-limited, source-preserving, separately authorized, correctable, and non-transitive by default.</p> <h3>7.5 Debt settlement and debt-for-stewardship arrangement</h3> <p>Debt relief may support stewardship only through informed agreement or lawful public process. Distress may not be exploited to obtain under-valued assets, waive claims, or coerce cultural or political conformity.</p> <h3>7.6 Reparations, restitution, and rematriation</h3> <p>These routes respond to wrongful dispossession, extraction, cultural harm, or continuing injustice. They require the affected nation or claimant’s authority, a claims process, evidence, competing-claims handling, remedy design, title and use consequences, continuity, and appeal.</p> <p>Rematriation means return into a life-affirming relationship and authority chosen by the affected people. It does not require transfer to a generic BAZ, global council, or framework-created trust.</p> <h3>7.7 Public-law acquisition or regulation</h3> <p>A participating jurisdiction may use public acquisition, land reform, conservation covenant, zoning, compulsory purchase, or another lawful instrument only under its own constituting law, public purpose, necessity, proportionality, compensation rules, hearing, appeal, and service-continuity protections.</p> <p>Hearthstone supplies no independent compulsory-acquisition power.</p> <h2>8. Claims and affected-party constitution</h2> <p>Before title, trust, covenant, or stewardship is changed, the case must identify:</p> <ul><li>affected Indigenous nations and their own institutions;</li> <li>present and historical titleholders;</li> <li>customary owners and users;</li> <li>occupants, residents, tenants, leaseholders, and licence holders;</li> <li>workers, contractors, unions, pension beneficiaries, and dependents;</li> <li>creditors, lienholders, insurers, and public obligations;</li> <li>ecological guardians and rights-holders where lawfully constituted;</li> <li>public-service and essential-resource dependencies;</li> <li>neighbouring and transboundary interests;</li> <li>future, child, disability, animal-welfare, and ecological interests requiring representation.</li></ul> <p>A claimant list does not decide standing. A stakeholder map does not equal consent. Lack of documentary title does not erase customary or occupancy evidence. Ecological urgency does not erase process.</p> <h2>9. Indigenous nation and rematriation boundary</h2> <h3>9.1 No proxy sovereignty</h3> <p>No Earth Council, global Indigenous seat, percentage of Indigenous membership, external elder, BAZ label, PHC, NGO, or Hearthstone office may:</p> <ul><li>provide FPIC for an affected nation;</li> <li>recognize the nation’s representatives;</li> <li>disclose sacred knowledge;</li> <li>determine territorial boundaries;</li> <li>waive restitution or title claims;</li> <li>choose the receiving legal entity;</li> <li>impose a stewardship form.</li></ul> <p>Such bodies may offer resources, solidarity, expert support, process audit, or a forum when invited.</p> <h3>9.2 Internal plurality</h3> <p>Where representation is contested, Hearthstone records the contest and follows the nation’s own law and any applicable independent legal process. It does not select the politically convenient representative.</p> <h3>9.3 Non-participation</h3> <p>A nation or community may refuse Hearthstone mechanisms without losing its claims, services, funding eligibility under other law, or right to pursue another route.</p> <h2>10. Sacred, cultural, and biocultural relations</h2> <p>Sacred or biocultural protection may involve several independent legal effects:</p> <ul><li>confidential internal recognition;</li> <li>restrictions on access or disclosure;</li> <li>cultural-heritage protection;</li> <li>ecological covenant or land-use restriction;</li> <li>title or rematriation claim;</li> <li>guardianship or representation;</li> <li>restoration or reparations.</li></ul> <p>These effects must not be collapsed into one designation.</p> <p>A petition threshold such as a number or percentage of signatories may open review but cannot authenticate sacredness, establish affected-nation authority, transfer title, or authorize enforcement.</p> <h2>11. Title, tenure, trust, and conveyance</h2> <h3>11.1 Verified baseline</h3> <p>No transition may proceed from a dashboard or aspirational registry. <code>HSP-12</code> must identify the legally operative interests and known disputes.</p> <h3>11.2 Stewardship entity does not automatically replace title</h3> <p>A stewardship trust may:</p> <ul><li>hold legal title;</li> <li>hold a lease, covenant, licence, beneficial interest, management mandate, or purpose right;</li> <li>operate an enterprise whose shares are held by another entity;</li> <li>coexist with customary or public title;</li> <li>serve as an administrative vehicle without owning the underlying ecosystem.</li></ul> <p>The exact relationship must be stated.</p> <h3>11.3 No silent extinguishment</h3> <p>Creation of a trust, commons registry, ecological right, sacred designation, or stewardship plan does not silently extinguish mortgages, tenancies, worker claims, customary uses, licences, easements, pensions, public duties, or competing claims.</p> <h3>11.4 Conveyance package</h3> <p>A complete conveyance package includes:</p> <ul><li>parties and authority;</li> <li>title and boundary;</li> <li>instrument and effective date;</li> <li>consideration or reparative basis;</li> <li>conditions precedent;</li> <li>residual rights and liabilities;</li> <li>tenant, worker, user, and service continuity;</li> <li>tax, debt, pension, and creditor treatment;</li> <li>registry instructions;</li> <li>challenge, correction, and reversal conditions.</li></ul> <h2>12. Stewardship trusts and commons entities</h2> <h3>12.1 Charter requirements</h3> <p>Every stewardship entity must state:</p> <ul><li>legal form and jurisdiction;</li> <li>asset or mission;</li> <li>purpose and beneficiaries;</li> <li>relationship to title;</li> <li>enumerated powers;</li> <li>prohibited acts;</li> <li>ecological and social duties;</li> <li>affected-nation authority and protected matters;</li> <li>governance and representation;</li> <li>finance, distributions, reserves, and audit;</li> <li>amendment and review;</li> <li>guardian relationship;</li> <li>suspension, succession, insolvency, exit, and dissolution;</li> <li>destination of title and assets at termination.</li></ul> <h3>12.2 Prohibited acts</h3> <p>Unless separately authorized, a stewardship entity may not:</p> <ul><li>alienate an inalienable asset;</li> <li>mortgage or pledge protected assets outside charter limits;</li> <li>evict residents or terminate essential access without applicable law and process;</li> <li>disclose protected knowledge;</li> <li>use ecological or social metrics as loyalty tests;</li> <li>exercise criminal, police, border, intelligence, or general regulatory power;</li> <li>claim to represent an affected Indigenous nation;</li> <li>convert a guardian into an owner or executive sovereign.</li></ul> <h3>12.3 Governance patterns</h3> <p>The Commons Governance Pattern Library may offer worker-community, resident-land, ecosystem-support, cooperative-enterprise, public-commons, and digital-commons patterns. A pattern is a template, not legal adoption or evidence of suitability.</p> <h2>13. Participation, representation, and youth</h2> <p>Youth, workers, residents, affected communities, and future-generations advocates should hold meaningful roles. Hearthstone does not create universal fixed seat percentages or automatic vetoes.</p> <p>A charter may establish reserved seats, consent domains, qualified majorities, delay-and-reconsider powers, published dissent, or independent review where lawful and contextually justified.</p> <p>Affected-nation authority is not reducible to a seat count.</p> <h2>14. Workers, residents, tenants, users, and services</h2> <p>No commons transition is complete until it addresses:</p> <ul><li>continued occupancy and anti-displacement protections;</li> <li>affordability and accessibility;</li> <li>water, sanitation, healthcare, food, energy, mobility, communications, and emergency access;</li> <li>wages, benefits, pensions, collective bargaining, retraining, and worker ownership;</li> <li>safe refusal to participate in unlawful or unsafe conversion activity;</li> <li>contractor and informal-worker interests;</li> <li>community facilities and public-service obligations;</li> <li>grievance and remedy.</li></ul> <p>For workers and contractors, <strong>addressing</strong> these interests means identifying their competent legal, contractual, collective-bargaining, pension, regulatory, or other source; preserving them through the transition; and routing unresolved duties to the competent owner. Hearthstone does not determine the general wage floor, employment status, dismissal standard, severance entitlement, union-recognition rule, bargaining procedure, statutory working time, occupational-safety code, or unemployment entitlement.</p> <p>A transition may improve governance while still harming people through disruption. Those harms count.</p> <h2>15. Finance, valuation, dividends, and incentives</h2> <h3>15.1 Valuation pluralism</h3> <p>Financial value, ecological condition, livelihood dependence, replacement cost, cultural relation, and reparative significance must be recorded separately. Non-market value does not provide a mathematically objective purchase price.</p> <h3>15.2 Voluntary incentives</h3> <p>Tax benefits, grants, guarantees, transition bonds, technical assistance, Hearts, Leaves, honours, and preferential programmes may be offered only by the competent authority and with transparent eligibility, anti-gaming rules, review, correction, and appeal.</p> <h3>15.3 Public financial action</h3> <p>Taxes, levies, mandatory reparations, tariffs, freezes, clawbacks, escrow, forfeiture, redistribution, and funding conditions require their own <code>CERGTA-45</code> or <code>CERGTA-46</code> route.</p> <p>A BHI, LMCI, Love Ledger, Proof of Care, or transition-readiness score cannot automatically move money.</p> <h3>15.4 Commons dividends</h3> <p>A stewardship entity may adopt a distribution policy, but Hearthstone does not prescribe a universal 40/30/30 formula, exchange rate, crawling peg, or Tobin tax. Distribution must account for maintenance, ecological duties, workers, residents, public obligations, reserves, debt, affected-nation agreements, and beneficiaries.</p> <h2>16. Ecological and social evidence</h2> <p>Hearthstone receives ecological evidence through EIOI v2.7 and planetary-health synthesis through PHG v0.9.</p> <p>Every use must record:</p> <ul><li>method and version;</li> <li>purpose and permitted use;</li> <li>spatial and temporal fit;</li> <li>uncertainty and dissent;</li> <li>distributional effects;</li> <li>baseline and counterfactual;</li> <li>external shocks;</li> <li>affected-nation and community interpretation;</li> <li>correction status;</li> <li>prohibited inferences.</li></ul> <p>LMCI, spiritual well-being, cultural vitality, or cohesion evidence may support voluntary learning. It cannot diagnose a community, create title defects, establish breach, or justify adverse action.</p> <h2>17. Performance, correction, revocation, and succession</h2> <h3>17.1 Performance review</h3> <p>A stewardship review is not an investigation or adjudication. It asks whether charter duties and plans are being met, what evidence supports the conclusion, what external conditions matter, and what support or correction is appropriate.</p> <h3>17.2 No automatic escalation</h3> <p>A threshold breach may open review. It cannot automatically:</p> <ul><li>remove stewards;</li> <li>appoint receivers;</li> <li>transfer title;</li> <li>freeze funding;</li> <li>revoke permits;</li> <li>blacklist actors;</li> <li>impose sanctions.</li></ul> <h3>17.3 Correction and capacity support</h3> <p>Where lawful and safe, the preferred sequence is:</p> <ol><li>notice and evidence access;</li> <li>steward and community response;</li> <li>independent verification;</li> <li>no-fault and structural analysis;</li> <li>voluntary correction and capacity support;</li> <li>time-bound review;</li> <li>separate referral if consequential action remains necessary.</li></ol> <h3>17.4 Suspension, revocation, and replacement</h3> <p>A competent authority may act only through <code>CERGTA-34</code>, with reasons, notice, hearing, interim relief, proportionality, service continuity, succession, registry correction, and remedy.</p> <h2>18. Urgent ecological protection and temporary receivership</h2> <h3>18.1 Urgent protection</h3> <p>Hearthstone may identify an imminent threat and route a minimum-necessary referral. It cannot itself authorize entry, seizure, closure, control of operations, or security action.</p> <h3>18.2 Receivership</h3> <p>Temporary ecological or stewardship receivership requires a separate <code>CERGTA-43</code> record specifying:</p> <ul><li>competent authority and legal source;</li> <li>individualized facts;</li> <li>exact asset and powers;</li> <li>less restrictive alternatives;</li> <li>essential-service and worker continuity;</li> <li>conflicts and independence;</li> <li>reporting and financial controls;</li> <li>review and interim relief;</li> <li>automatic expiry;</li> <li>return to ordinary governance;</li> <li>compensation and remedy if wrongful.</li></ul> <p>An emergency declaration does not automatically establish these elements.</p> <h3>18.3 No-fault support</h3> <p>Disaster, cross-border pollution, inherited damage, and other external shocks may justify grants, technical assistance, insurance, or temporary support without classifying stewards as deficient.</p> <h2>19. Registry and public record</h2> <p>The Commons and Stewardship Registry is a record system, not a title sovereign.</p> <p>It must:</p> <ul><li>cite the legally effective source;</li> <li>distinguish proposed, disputed, suspended, and final status;</li> <li>preserve correction and supersession;</li> <li>allow offline and accessible participation;</li> <li>protect personal, commercial, cultural, and sacred information;</li> <li>avoid publishing sensitive ecological locations;</li> <li>propagate corrections;</li> <li>support lawful deletion and protected archive.</li></ul> <p>Blockchain may be used as one integrity mechanism. Immutability cannot override correction, privacy, Indigenous data law, court order, or legal title.</p> <h3>19.1 Person-continuity and registry boundary</h3> <p>A Commons and Stewardship Registry may hold purpose-limited references to persons or roles necessary for title, claim, stewardship, guardianship, tenancy, creditor, worker, beneficiary, or transition administration. Those references are not a civil registry or universal identity layer.</p> <p>Where two records are asserted to concern the same person:</p> <ul><li>the specific legal/administrative purpose and authority must be recorded;</li> <li>only minimum necessary attributes may cross the boundary;</li> <li>pairwise recognition is non-transitive by default;</li> <li>source records remain distinct;</li> <li>mistaken merge, mistaken split, duplicate, collision, correction, and unlinking must be supported;</li> <li>a continuity assertion does not establish title, standing, heirship, citizenship, migration status, family relation, legal capacity, or unrelated eligibility.</li></ul> <p>Where an externally established death/status event affects title, trust, claim, occupancy, stewardship, or beneficiary administration, Hearthstone preserves unresolved claims, remedies, belongings, service continuity, accrued obligations, and succession questions until the competent authority resolves them. Receipt of the event does not itself authorize transfer, eviction, exclusion, title change, trustee replacement, or dissolution.</p> <p>HSP-33 may carry the domain registry correction; CIS/0.2.4 Appendix M supplies the constitutional minimum where the correction or linkage crosses person records or frameworks.</p> <h2>20. Enforcement and security boundary</h2> <p>Hearthstone v2.0 retires reliance on the former Global Enforcement Task Force and any theory that framework participation or refusal directly triggers tariffs, indictments, arrest warrants, or physical enforcement.</p> <p>A competent court or authority may issue an order under separate law. <code>HSP-38</code> records the handoff and acceptance.</p> <p>Where execution involves search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, intelligence, cyber action, border action, asset freeze, or other coercive power, the executor must separately satisfy SCPA. Hearthstone supplies neither mission command nor operational authority.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>A commons claim may justify a hearing. It does not authorize a raid.</strong></p></blockquote> <h2>21. Communications, culture, and public learning</h2> <p>The Story of Stewardship, art residencies, mentorship, curricula, simulations, and public toolkits may support cultural transition.</p> <p>They must not:</p> <ul><li>misrepresent contested claims as settled;</li> <li>shame non-participants;</li> <li>target developmental or spiritual profiles;</li> <li>use counter-disinformation work as surveillance or censorship;</li> <li>condition rights on ideological agreement;</li> <li>publish protected knowledge or personal stories without consent.</li></ul> <p>Former owners, workers, residents, and communities may experience grief, uncertainty, or disagreement without being classified as obstacles to progress.</p> <h2>22. Pilots and sandbox zones</h2> <p>Pilots may test:</p> <ul><li>voluntary conversion forms;</li> <li>governance charters;</li> <li>legal-aid and counselling;</li> <li>worker, tenant, and service safeguards;</li> <li>offline registries;</li> <li>stewardship planning;</li> <li>financing and transition support;</li> <li>monitoring and learning.</li></ul> <p>Pilots may not test unactivated powers such as compulsory conversion, title extinguishment, automatic sacred designation, tax, tariff, sanction, freeze, receivership, eviction, seizure, or arrest.</p> <p>A target such as “25% of assets stewarded” is a learning metric, not a mandate to reach the percentage.</p> <h2>23. Complaints, review, and remedy</h2> <p>Every consequential pathway must provide:</p> <ul><li>accessible notice and reasons;</li> <li>language, disability, cultural, and digital-access support;</li> <li>counsel or qualified assistance where stakes require it;</li> <li>evidence access subject to protected-knowledge safeguards;</li> <li>correction and reply;</li> <li>independent review and appeal;</li> <li>interim relief before irreversible harm;</li> <li>registry correction;</li> <li>restitution, compensation, or requalification where appropriate;</li> <li>whistleblower and retaliation protection.</li></ul> <p>The Stewardship Ombudsman may assist and investigate service complaints within a lawfully defined mandate. It cannot adjudicate title or impose sanctions.</p> <h2>24. Exit, dissolution, and authority return</h2> <p>A complete exit or dissolution plan addresses:</p> <ul><li>title and asset destination;</li> <li>outstanding claims and litigation;</li> <li>residents, tenants, users, workers, pensions, and services;</li> <li>debt, tax, contracts, and public obligations;</li> <li>guardian and affected-nation relationships;</li> <li>permits and licences;</li> <li>funds and reserves;</li> <li>registry and public notice;</li> <li>data, archives, and protected knowledge;</li> <li>successor governance;</li> <li>return of office, receiver, credential, and delegated authority.</li></ul> <p>A BAZ or jurisdiction may leave a GGF arrangement without punitive forfeiture merely for leaving. Existing lawful rights and obligations continue according to applicable law.</p> <h2>25. Canonical Hearthstone operational records</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HSP-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Scope, Adoption, and Non-Authority Register</td><td>Pins the exact instruments, jurisdiction, adoption state, delegations, exclusions, sunset, and powers Hearthstone does not possess.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-02</td><td>Commons Asset or Enterprise Intake</td><td>Identifies the asset, enterprise, land, infrastructure, capital, data, or cultural resource proposed for transition without creating a claim, designation, title defect, or obligation.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-03</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Workers, Tenants, and Users Map</td><td>Identifies all materially affected interests, essential dependencies, accessibility needs, representation limits, and notice routes.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-04</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>Identifies the specific nation or nations, their own law and institutions, territorial relationship, consent requirements, internal plurality, and protected knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-05</td><td>Voluntary Transition Inquiry and Advice Record</td><td>Records a non-binding request for information, options, counselling, and technical assistance; inquiry creates no commitment or adverse inference.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-06</td><td>Claim Intake and Relief Requested</td><td>Records each claimant, standing basis, remedy sought, evidence offered, confidentiality needs, and whether the claim concerns title, use, occupation, reparations, sacred relation, or stewardship.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-07</td><td>Competing Claims and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>Preserves all competing claims, current uses, public interests, tenancy, labour, customary rights, debt, and unresolved representation questions.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-08</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Package</td><td>Separates evidence from legal conclusion and records provenance, contest, uncertainty, translation, and protected-knowledge limits.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-09</td><td>Interim Anti-Dissipation and Non-Prejudice Request</td><td>Requests time-limited lawful protection against asset stripping or irreversible ecological harm without deciding ownership or final relief.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-10</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, and Biocultural Relation Petition</td><td>Records the affected nation or community’s own petition, protected elements, access rules, non-disclosure limits, and requested legal consequences.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-11</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, and Biocultural Designation Decision Interface</td><td>Links only to a competent CERGTA-21 decision; Hearthstone cannot authenticate sacredness or designate on behalf of another nation.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-12</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Licence, Covenant, and Encumbrance Baseline</td><td>Creates a verified pre-transition legal baseline, including public, private, customary, collective, leasehold, mortgage, lien, licence, and service interests.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-13</td><td>Independent Valuation and Non-Market Value Record</td><td>Records monetary valuation, replacement cost, livelihood value, ecological and cultural non-market value, uncertainty, and prohibited uses of the valuation.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-14</td><td>Transition Options and Alternatives Assessment</td><td>Compares no-change, covenant, lease, cooperative, trust, public purchase, donation, inheritance, debt settlement, reparations, rematriation, and other lawful routes.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-15</td><td>Consent, Authorization, and Representation Record</td><td>Records exactly who may authorize which element, free and informed consent where required, withdrawal terms, representation boundaries, and unresolved objections.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-16</td><td>Public-Law Acquisition, Rematriation, or Reparations Basis</td><td>Identifies any lawful expropriation, restitution, reparations, treaty, court, or public-law route separately from voluntary conversion.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-17</td><td>Conveyance, Trust Constitution, Cooperative Conversion, or Rematriation Authorization</td><td>Records the exact legal act, parties, conditions, effective date, registrable instrument, residual interests, and limits.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-18</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, and Distribution Decision</td><td>Records valuation method, eligible recipients, compensation or reparations basis, benefit sharing, escrow, review, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-19</td><td>Debt, Pension, Tax, Creditor, and Capital-Structure Resolution</td><td>Prevents conversion from silently impairing debt, pensions, creditors, taxes, or financial stability and records lawful settlement or assumption.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-20</td><td>Tenant, Resident, User, and Essential-Service Continuity Plan</td><td>Protects housing, water, sanitation, healthcare, energy, food, accessibility, transport, and other essential uses during and after transition.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-21</td><td>Worker, Contractor, and Livelihood Transition Plan</td><td>Preserves and routes wages, benefits, collective bargaining, pensions, retraining, safe refusal, immigration status, and worker representation under their competent legal, contractual, collective, or regulatory sources; the record does not constitute ordinary employment rights.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-22</td><td>Stewardship Trust or Commons Entity Charter</td><td>Defines purpose, legal form, beneficiaries, powers, prohibited acts, asset lock, amendment, review, dissolution, and relationship to title.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-23</td><td>Stakeholder Governance and Representation Constitution</td><td>Defines selection, recall, terms, conflicts, quorum, minority rights, affected-nation authority, youth participation, worker and user representation, and transparency.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-24</td><td>Ecological Guardian Interface</td><td>Links to a separately appointed guardian under CERGTA-16–19 and prevents the stakeholder body, Earth Council, PHC, or Hearthstone office from self-appointing.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-25</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>Defines lawful objectives, baseline, measures, budget, responsibilities, community participation, essential minimums, and revision schedule.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-26</td><td>Finance, Dividend, Reserve, and Benefit-Sharing Plan</td><td>Defines revenue, reinvestment, distributions, reserves, public-fund conditions, worker and community shares, audit, and insolvency protection.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-27</td><td>Monitoring and Performance Evidence Package</td><td>Receives versioned EIOI evidence and records context, uncertainty, external shocks, distributions, dissent, and prohibited automatic consequences.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-28</td><td>Stewardship Performance Review</td><td>Provides a reasoned review with notice, answer rights, no-fault analysis, independent verification, and separation of metric evidence from legal breach.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-29</td><td>Voluntary Correction and Capacity-Support Plan</td><td>Offers resources, training, mediation, finance, governance repair, and measurable correction without presuming blame or surrender of rights.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-30</td><td>Stewardship Suspension, Revocation, or Replacement Referral</td><td>Routes a proposed consequential change to CERGTA-34 and competent law; no threshold, score, or office decision may self-execute.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-31</td><td>Succession, Exit, Reversion, and Dissolution Plan</td><td>Defines successor stewards, member exit, BAZ or jurisdictional exit, trust dissolution, title destination, service continuity, claims, archive, and authority return; person-level heirship or beneficiary succession remains with the competent external authority.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-32</td><td>Commons and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>Publishes only legally effective facts with provenance, access controls, non-conclusive status for disputed matters, and offline accessibility.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-33</td><td>Registry Challenge, Collision, and Correction</td><td>Allows correction of registry identity, boundary, title, guardian, charter, permit, and status errors with downstream propagation; cross-framework person continuity follows CIS/0.2.4 and does not create general civil identity.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-34</td><td>Permit, Allocation, Licence, Tariff, and Standard Interface</td><td>Records external CERGTA-35–38 decisions affecting a stewarded asset without allowing the trust or registry to create them.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-35</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Referral</td><td>Packages minimum facts for CERGTA-42 or ECRC/CDEE; it does not authorize entry, seizure, closure, receivership, or security action.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-36</td><td>Temporary Receivership and Administration Interface</td><td>Accepts only a separately authorized CERGTA-43 record with narrow scope, service continuity, independent review, expiry, and restoration of ordinary governance.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-37</td><td>Tax, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, Grant, and Funding Interface</td><td>Separates voluntary incentives from taxes, levies, funding conditions, freezes, reparations funds, and other public financial action.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-38</td><td>Adjudication and Enforcement Handoff</td><td>Routes disputes and orders to competent CERGTA-47/48 bodies and SCPA where execution involves search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber, border, or financial coercion.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-39</td><td>Complaint, Appeal, Interim Relief, and Remedy</td><td>Provides complaints, accessibility, counsel, review, registry correction, restitution, compensation, and independent appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-40</td><td>Data, Traditional Knowledge, and Archive Disposition</td><td>Defines return, deletion, retention, public memory, model-training prohibition, sacred-knowledge protection, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-41</td><td>Pilot, Sandbox, and Power Non-Activation Register</td><td>Ensures pilots test forms, support, governance, and learning without creating title, receivership, sanctions, tariffs, or automatic conversion.</td></tr><tr><td>HSP-42</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Service, and Duty Custody</td><td>Keeps unresolved claims and duties visible after closure without preserving expired authority or blocking essential services.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>26. CERGTA common header inheritance</h2> <p>Every consequential Hearthstone record contains or explicitly inherits all forty <code>CERGTA/0.1</code> common fields.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Inherited common field</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>record_id</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>record_instance_id</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>record_version</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>status</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>created_at</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>effective_at</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>created_by</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>competent_authority</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>legal_source</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>authority_activation_state</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>ecological_action_cluster_id</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>action_class</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>exact_act</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>entity_asset_resource_id</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>ecological_and_legal_boundary_version</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>affected_persons_and_communities</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>affected_indigenous_nations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>claimants_and_interest_holders</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>linked_records</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>source_and_reliability_limits</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>uncertainty_and_dissent</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>prohibited_inferences</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>rights_and_safeguards</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>essential_minimums</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>consent_fpic_and_nonengagement_status</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>access_class</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>notice_status</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>review_authority</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>review_due_at</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>expires_at_or_nonexpiry_basis</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>challenge_and_interim_relief_route</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>termination_trigger</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>authority_asset_and_credential_return</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>data_disposition_rule</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>remedy_route</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>supersedes</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>superseded_by</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>integrity_digest</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>public_summary_or_protected_basis</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>An implementation may combine records in one packet only if each canonical identity, authority, access class, review clock, expiry, closure condition, and remedy remains distinct.</p> <h2>27. Complete CERGTA crosswalk</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>CERGTA</th><th>Canonical record</th><th>Class</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Hearthstone record</th><th>Conformance note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Legal Source, Jurisdiction, and Activation Status</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-01</td><td>Constituting instrument, adoption, jurisdiction, and non-authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-02</td><td>Ecological Entity Candidate, Resource, Asset, and Boundary Intake</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-02</td><td>Commons asset and enterprise intake.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-03</td><td>Observation, Monitoring, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>A</td><td>imports</td><td>EIOI observation records via HSP-27</td><td>Hearthstone does not originate ecological fact by registry or transition request.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-04</td><td>Ecological Data Purpose, Consent, Sovereignty, Access, and Retention Record</td><td>A</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-40</td><td>Data, consent, sovereignty, access, retention, and archive.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-05</td><td>Indicator, Index, Threshold, Weighting, and Model Specification</td><td>B</td><td>imports</td><td>EIOI methods via HSP-27</td><td>No Hearthstone-created BHI/LMCI or risk score may create consequence.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-06</td><td>Ecological Interpretation, Causal Assessment, and Uncertainty Record</td><td>B</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-27/HSP-28</td><td>Contextual interpretation and no-fault analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-07</td><td>Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>B</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-04/HSP-08/HSP-10/HSP-40</td><td>Protected knowledge and affected-nation control.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-08</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Rights Map</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-03</td><td>Affected persons, nations, workers, tenants, users, and essential dependencies.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>F</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-04</td><td>Affected Indigenous nation and applicable law.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-10</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Support Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-03/HSP-15/HSP-39</td><td>Notice, participation, accessibility, counsel, support, challenge.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-11</td><td>Ecological Rights Review Petition and Standing Record</td><td>C</td><td>splits</td><td>HSP-06/HSP-10</td><td>A rights or designation petition may be routed, but Hearthstone does not determine standing.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-12</td><td>Rights Review Recommendation and Dissent Record</td><td>C</td><td>splits</td><td>HSP-08/HSP-10</td><td>Evidence and advisory submissions only.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-13</td><td>Lawful Ecological Rights Recognition Decision</td><td>D</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-13</td><td>No ecosystem-rights recognition authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation Record</td><td>D</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-14</td><td>No rights catalogue or conflict-priority authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-15</td><td>Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution Record</td><td>D</td><td>imports</td><td>external CERGTA-15 through HSP-02/HSP-32</td><td>Hearthstone records, but does not constitute, legal ecological boundaries.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-16</td><td>Guardian Nomination and Eligibility Record</td><td>E</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-16</td><td>No guardian nomination by Hearthstone or its offices.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-17</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, and Standing Record</td><td>E</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-24</td><td>Only a separately competent appointment may be recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-18</td><td>Guardian Fiduciary Duty, Instructions, Conflict, and Recusal Record</td><td>E</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-24</td><td>Guardian duties and instructions remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-19</td><td>Guardian Review, Complaint, Removal, Substitution, and Liability Record</td><td>E</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-24/HSP-39</td><td>Guardian complaints and removal remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-20</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Petition</td><td>F</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-10</td><td>Petition and protected relation record.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-21</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Decision</td><td>F</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-11</td><td>Only a competent designation decision has legal effect.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-22</td><td>Claim Intake, Standing, and Relief Requested Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-06</td><td>Claim intake and relief requested.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-23</td><td>Competing Claims, Parties, Interests, and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-07</td><td>Competing claims and non-extinguishment map.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-24</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-08</td><td>Historical, customary, occupancy, use, and documentary evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-25</td><td>Interim Non-Prejudice and Anti-Dissipation Protection Record</td><td>G</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-09</td><td>Time-limited anti-dissipation request; no final title inference.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-26</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Covenant, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-12</td><td>Title, tenure, trust, licence, covenant, and encumbrance baseline.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-27</td><td>Transfer, Rematriation, Trust Constitution, and Conveyance Authorization</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-16/HSP-17</td><td>Lawful transfer, trust constitution, cooperative conversion, or rematriation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-28</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, Debt, Tenant, Worker, User, and Service Continuity Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-18–HSP-21</td><td>Compensation, debt, tenant, worker, user, and service continuity.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-29</td><td>Commons, Title, Rights, Guardian, Permit, and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-32</td><td>Legally effective registry entry with dispute flags.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-30</td><td>Registry Error, Identity Collision, Boundary Conflict, and Correction Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-33</td><td>Registry correction and downstream propagation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-31</td><td>Stewardship Charter, Purpose, Powers, Duties, and Prohibited Acts</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-22/HSP-23</td><td>Stewardship charter, powers, duties, and prohibited acts.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-32</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-25/HSP-26</td><td>Stewardship plan, budget, finance, and participation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-33</td><td>Stewardship Performance, Audit, Metric-Gaming, and Context Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-27/HSP-28</td><td>Performance evidence and review with context and anti-gaming safeguards.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-34</td><td>Stewardship Correction, Suspension, Revocation, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-29–HSP-31</td><td>Correction, suspension, revocation, succession, and dissolution through due process.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-35</td><td>Ecological Standard, Target, Plan, and Legal Adoption Status Record</td><td>J</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-34</td><td>Standards and plans retain external adoption status.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-36</td><td>Resource Priority, Essential Minimum, Allocation Method, and Scarcity Record</td><td>K</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-20/HSP-25/HSP-34</td><td>Essential minimum and scarcity protections.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-37</td><td>Quota, Permit, Licence, Tariff, Bond, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>K</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-34</td><td>Quota, permit, licence, tariff, bond, and use conditions are externally authorized.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-38</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Service Continuity, and Non-Deprivation Record</td><td>K</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-20/HSP-34</td><td>Commercial restrictions cannot interrupt essential minimums.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-39</td><td>Inspection Authorization, Entry, Sampling, and Report Record</td><td>L</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-39</td><td>Hearthstone has no compulsory inspection power.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-40</td><td>Ecological Investigation Opening, Methods, Parties, and Closure Record</td><td>L</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-40</td><td>Hearthstone performance review is not legal investigation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-41</td><td>Ecological Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, and Admissibility Record</td><td>L</td><td>splits</td><td>HSP-08/HSP-27/HSP-38</td><td>Evidence packages are preserved; legal custody and admissibility external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-42</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>M</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-35</td><td>Urgent referral only; authorization external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-43</td><td>Temporary Ecological Receivership and Administration Record</td><td>N</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-36</td><td>Receivership requires separate authority, expiry, review, and return.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-44</td><td>Port, Market, Procurement, Certification, Listing, and Delisting Restriction Record</td><td>N</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-34/HSP-38</td><td>Port, market, procurement, certification, listing, and delisting are external decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-45</td><td>Ecological Tax, Tariff, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, and Funding Decision</td><td>O</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-26/HSP-37</td><td>Financial plan and public financial action remain distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-46</td><td>Freeze, Escrow, Forfeiture, Redistribution, Reparations, and Compensation Fund Record</td><td>O</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-18/HSP-37</td><td>Freeze, escrow, forfeiture, redistribution, reparations, and compensation funds require separate authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-47</td><td>Ecological and Commons Adjudication, Judgment, and Reasons Record</td><td>P</td><td>imports</td><td>HSP-38/HSP-39</td><td>Adjudication and judgment remain independent.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-48</td><td>Enforcement Request, Acceptance, Execution, and SCPA Interface Record</td><td>P</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-38</td><td>Enforcement request, acceptance, execution, and SCPA interface.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-49</td><td>Ecological Restoration, Corrective Action, Requalification, and Verification Record</td><td>Q</td><td>extends</td><td>HSP-25/HSP-29/HSP-30</td><td>Restoration and requalification require separate verification and lawful decision.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-50</td><td>Appeal, Interim Relief, Complaint, Correction, Restitution, and Compensation Record</td><td>Q</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-33/HSP-39</td><td>Appeal, interim relief, complaint, correction, restitution, and compensation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-51</td><td>Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-31</td><td>Authority expiry, return, succession, exit, and dissolution.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-52</td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, Public Memory, and Protected-Knowledge Disposition Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-40</td><td>Data return, deletion, archive, public memory, and protected-knowledge disposition.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-53</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-42</td><td>Unresolved duty without residual power.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-54</td><td>Ecological and Commons Power Non-Activation, Pilot, and Sandbox Register</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>HSP-41</td><td>Pilots and sandboxes do not activate consequential powers.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>28. Readiness gates</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Title</th><th>Pass condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>G01</td><td>Constituting authority</td><td>Every consequential act identifies current law, competent authority, jurisdiction, adoption state, limits, review, expiry, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>G02</td><td>Voluntary–compulsory separation</td><td>Donation, sale, inheritance, cooperative conversion, public acquisition, reparations, and rematriation are never presented as one generic transition route.</td></tr><tr><td>G03</td><td>Affected-nation authority</td><td>The specific affected Indigenous nation controls its own consent, law, sacred knowledge, representation, and territorial relationship.</td></tr><tr><td>G04</td><td>No global proxy consent</td><td>Earth Council or any global body cannot consent, designate sacredness, transfer title, or waive another nation’s rights.</td></tr><tr><td>G05</td><td>Claims constitution</td><td>All claims, competing claims, occupancy, use, tenancy, labour, customary rights, and public interests are identified before consequential title action.</td></tr><tr><td>G06</td><td>Evidence–decision separation</td><td>Historical, ecological, cultural, citizen, AI, and registry evidence is evaluated but does not self-execute.</td></tr><tr><td>G07</td><td>Sacredness–title separation</td><td>Sacred or biocultural recognition does not itself convey title, cancel interests, appoint guardians, or authorize enforcement.</td></tr><tr><td>G08</td><td>Rights–title separation</td><td>Ecological personhood does not itself transfer property, create a trust, or decide compensation.</td></tr><tr><td>G09</td><td>Verified title baseline</td><td>Every transition starts from a current title, tenure, trust, licence, covenant, debt, and encumbrance baseline.</td></tr><tr><td>G10</td><td>Valid authorization</td><td>Each party’s authority, representation boundary, consent, and withdrawal conditions are documented.</td></tr><tr><td>G11</td><td>Compensation and reparations</td><td>Compensation, reparations, debt, pensions, creditors, and public liabilities are resolved through a reasoned and appealable route.</td></tr><tr><td>G12</td><td>Tenant and user continuity</td><td>No transition silently displaces residents, tenants, customary users, permit holders, or essential-service recipients.</td></tr><tr><td>G13</td><td>Worker transition</td><td>Wages, benefits, bargaining, pensions, immigration status, retraining, and safe refusal remain protected under their competent sources and are made transition-visible without creating Hearthstone employment-law authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G14</td><td>Trust charter precision</td><td>The legal entity, title relationship, purposes, powers, prohibited acts, beneficiaries, asset lock, amendment, and dissolution are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>G15</td><td>Governance legitimacy</td><td>Selection, recall, conflicts, minority rights, accessibility, affected-nation authority, youth participation, and worker/user representation are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>G16</td><td>Guardian independence</td><td>Guardians are separately nominated and appointed and neither own the asset nor acquire general governance power.</td></tr><tr><td>G17</td><td>Metric humility</td><td>BHI, LMCI, Love Ledger, Proof of Care, dashboards, AI, and certification scores may trigger review only.</td></tr><tr><td>G18</td><td>No-fault context</td><td>Wildfire, climate disaster, upstream pollution, war, market shocks, and inherited damage are separated from stewardship breach.</td></tr><tr><td>G19</td><td>Correction before displacement</td><td>Capacity support and correction are considered before suspension or replacement unless separate urgent authority exists.</td></tr><tr><td>G20</td><td>Revocation due process</td><td>Stewardship revocation or replacement requires notice, reasons, independent review, interim relief, succession, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>G21</td><td>Registry non-constitutive status</td><td>The registry records legally effective facts and disputed status; blockchain or data entry does not create title.</td></tr><tr><td>G22</td><td>Essential-resource firewall</td><td>Commercial or financial action cannot interrupt minimum water, sanitation, healthcare, food, housing, disability, energy, or emergency access.</td></tr><tr><td>G23</td><td>Urgent-protection boundary</td><td>Urgency triggers a separate CERGTA/ECRC route and cannot silently create receivership, title transfer, or enforcement.</td></tr><tr><td>G24</td><td>Receivership constitution</td><td>Temporary administration is narrow, reviewed, expiring, service-preserving, non-punitive, and returns authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G25</td><td>Financial-action separation</td><td>Incentives, grants, taxes, tariffs, levies, bonds, freezes, escrow, and reparations funds remain legally distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>G26</td><td>No enforcement by framework</td><td>Hearthstone, its transition offices, trusts, councils, registries, and guardians cannot search, seize, detain, use force, or impose external sanctions.</td></tr><tr><td>G27</td><td>SCPA interface</td><td>Any coercive execution uses separate SCPA authority, mission command, evidence custody, termination, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>G28</td><td>Pilot non-activation</td><td>Sandboxes test forms and support only; targets, percentages, milestones, or demonstrations do not activate power.</td></tr><tr><td>G29</td><td>Exit and dissolution</td><td>BAZ exit, trust dissolution, bankruptcy, jurisdictional withdrawal, and programme termination preserve claims, services, title clarity, and data duties.</td></tr><tr><td>G30</td><td>Complaint and remedy</td><td>Accessible complaints, counsel, interim relief, correction, restitution, compensation, and independent appeal exist.</td></tr><tr><td>G31</td><td>Data and knowledge disposition</td><td>Personal, commercial, cultural, sacred, and Traditional Knowledge data have purpose, access, retention, return, deletion, and archive rules.</td></tr><tr><td>G32</td><td>Unresolved duty without residual power</td><td>Open claims or harms remain visible after closure without extending expired receivership, restrictions, or office authority.</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>A deployment cannot claim Hearthstone conformance while any applicable gate is failed or untested. Advisory materials and capacity-building may continue when clearly labelled non-consequential.</p> <h2>29. Compound implementation tests</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected constitutional route</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T01</td><td>Voluntary farm donation with tenants</td><td>Owner offers land to a trust. HSP-03/12/14/15/17/20 ensure tenant and use rights are addressed before conveyance.</td></tr><tr><td>T02</td><td>Indigenous rematriation with two claimant nations</td><td>HSP-04/06/07/08 preserves both nations’ authority and blocks Earth Council substitution or generic BAZ transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>T03</td><td>Sacred-site petition without title consent</td><td>HSP-10/11 may protect knowledge and route designation; no title transfer occurs without HSP-12/15–18.</td></tr><tr><td>T04</td><td>River personhood adjacent to private land</td><td>CERGTA rights recognition is recorded, but HSP-12/17 separately govern land title and easements.</td></tr><tr><td>T05</td><td>Citizen map contains title error</td><td>HSP-02/08 treats the map as evidence; HSP-33 corrects the registry before consequence.</td></tr><tr><td>T06</td><td>BHI decline after wildfire</td><td>HSP-27/28 records no-fault shock; no automatic revocation or funding freeze occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>T07</td><td>Love Ledger spoofing allegation</td><td>The score is suspended for review; it does not itself establish fraud, breach, repayment, or replacement.</td></tr><tr><td>T08</td><td>Worker cooperative conversion</td><td>HSP-14/15/17/19/21 protect member authorization, debt, pensions, bargaining, and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td>T09</td><td>Multinational seeks legacy certification</td><td>Certification has no title or immunity effect; charter, liabilities, workers, and affected communities remain separately reviewed.</td></tr><tr><td>T10</td><td>Debt-for-stewardship swap</td><td>HSP-13/15/17–19 require valuation, informed authorization, creditor treatment, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>T11</td><td>Public acquisition for ecological restoration</td><td>HSP-16–20 distinguish public-law acquisition from voluntary conversion and protect compensation, tenants, workers, and services.</td></tr><tr><td>T12</td><td>Reparations transfer to affected nation</td><td>HSP-04/06–08/16–18 follow the nation’s own authority and do not route title through a generic BAZ.</td></tr><tr><td>T13</td><td>Conflicting occupancy and documentary title</td><td>HSP-07/08/12/39 preserve both claims and prevent registry finality before adjudication.</td></tr><tr><td>T14</td><td>Urgent logging threat</td><td>HSP-35 routes a CERGTA-42 request; Hearthstone cannot seize equipment or impose permanent closure.</td></tr><tr><td>T15</td><td>Emergency receivership after flood</td><td>HSP-36 requires separate authority, narrow scope, service continuity, early review, expiry, and return.</td></tr><tr><td>T16</td><td>Trust insolvency</td><td>HSP-19/20/21/26/31 protect creditors, workers, residents, services, and successor arrangements.</td></tr><tr><td>T17</td><td>Youth object to asset liquidation</td><td>HSP-23 requires structured review, reasons, dissent, and independent challenge; no universal automatic veto is presumed.</td></tr><tr><td>T18</td><td>Guardian conflicts with stakeholder council</td><td>HSP-24 preserves the guardian’s independent fiduciary route without giving either body title or enforcement power.</td></tr><tr><td>T19</td><td>Stewardship underperformance</td><td>HSP-28/29 precede HSP-30 unless a separate urgent authority applies.</td></tr><tr><td>T20</td><td>Malicious steward asset stripping</td><td>HSP-09 may seek interim anti-dissipation protection; investigation, seizure, and adjudication remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>T21</td><td>Commons registry uses blockchain</td><td>HSP-32 requires lawful source and correction; blockchain immutability cannot defeat appeal or deletion duties.</td></tr><tr><td>T22</td><td>Paper-only rural community</td><td>Offline records and Commons Scribes are accepted without reducing legal rights or access.</td></tr><tr><td>T23</td><td>Trade preference tied to ecological score</td><td>HSP-37 separates voluntary preference from tariff or market restriction; score alone cannot decide either.</td></tr><tr><td>T24</td><td>Non-participating state rejects model</td><td>Hearthstone offers diplomacy and voluntary cooperation only; it cannot trigger tariffs, arrest warrants, or force.</td></tr><tr><td>T25</td><td>Water infrastructure conversion</td><td>HSP-20 and CERGTA essential minimums prevent household or hospital interruption during title and governance change.</td></tr><tr><td>T26</td><td>Urban housing commons conversion</td><td>Residents, tenants, affordability, accessibility, maintenance, and eviction protections are resolved before transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>T27</td><td>Cultural archive proposed as commons</td><td>HSP-04/10/40 protect affected-nation and individual data; commons status does not make restricted knowledge public.</td></tr><tr><td>T28</td><td>BAZ exits the GGF</td><td>HSP-31 preserves lawful title, claims, services, records, and local succession without punitive reversion.</td></tr><tr><td>T29</td><td>Pilot meets 25% asset target</td><td>The milestone creates no power to convert remaining assets or declare the pilot successful without qualitative review.</td></tr><tr><td>T30</td><td>Court order requires physical execution</td><td>HSP-38 records acceptance; SCPA separately governs search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber, or financial coercion.</td></tr><tr><td>T31</td><td>Erroneous stewardship revocation</td><td>HSP-39/33 provide interim relief, reinstatement or requalification, compensation, and registry correction.</td></tr><tr><td>T32</td><td>Programme closes with unresolved claim</td><td>HSP-42 preserves the claim and service duty while HSP-31 returns all expired authority.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>30. Version transition from v1.5</h2> <h3>Preserved</h3> <ul><li>regenerative stewardship and commons vision;</li> <li>voluntary corporate and cooperative transition;</li> <li>Land Back, rematriation, reparations, inheritance, and debt-settlement pathways;</li> <li>transition finance, mentorship, education, pattern libraries, offline access, and public learning;</li> <li>just-transition support for workers and communities;</li> <li>stewardship review, correction, succession, and dissolution;</li> <li>local and bioregional implementation with transboundary cooperation.</li></ul> <h3>Reconstituted</h3> <ul><li>Stewardship Trusts are lawful forms with explicit title relationships, not automatic replacements for ownership.</li> <li>Bio-cultural arrangements follow the affected nation’s authority rather than mandatory PHC/Earth Council co-governance.</li> <li>Sacred designation is separated from title, guardianship, and enforcement.</li> <li>Rematriation is separated from voluntary sale, public acquisition, reparations, and compulsory legal process.</li> <li>The registry records lawfully effective facts and cannot create or perfect title by blockchain entry.</li> <li>BHI, LMCI, Love Ledger, Proof of Care, readiness scores, and AI monitoring may trigger review only.</li> <li>Stewardship correction, suspension, revocation, receivership, and succession use distinct records and authorities.</li> <li>youth and future-generations participation gains procedural weight without a universal automatic veto.</li> <li>transition offices support rather than rule.</li></ul> <h3>Retired</h3> <ul><li>the International Commons Court as a framework-created universal court;</li> <li>framework-created enforcement against non-participating nations;</li> <li>trade denial, tariffs, indictments, arrest warrants, or GETF protection arising directly from Hearthstone;</li> <li>Earth Council ratification of sacredness for affected peoples;</li> <li>automatic stewardship escalation or revocation from BHI/LMCI thresholds;</li> <li>emergency receivership triggered solely by crisis command;</li> <li>universal dividend percentages, exchange rules, or transaction taxes;</li> <li>mandatory blockchain or Love Ledger participation;</li> <li>asset-conversion targets as activation thresholds.</li></ul> <h2>31. Minimum implementation package</h2> <p>Before any consequential transition, an adopter must have:</p> <ol><li>an exact legal-adoption record;</li> <li>competent registries and adjudicative routes;</li> <li>affected-nation and claims procedures;</li> <li>tenant, worker, user, and essential-service protections;</li> <li>valuation, compensation, debt, tax, and pension capacity;</li> <li>independent legal aid and accessible complaints;</li> <li>CERGTA-compatible records and protected data systems;</li> <li>SCPA-compatible execution interfaces where coercive orders might arise;</li> <li>emergency interfaces for urgent ecological action;</li> <li>tested exit, dissolution, succession, and authority-return procedures.</li></ol> <h2>32. Closing doctrine</h2> <p>Hearthstone does not abandon the ambition to transform property. It makes that ambition governable.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>A commons worthy of trust cannot be built by treating claims, consent, title, stewardship, metrics, receivership, and enforcement as the same act.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>The protocol succeeds when people and ecosystems gain durable protection, affected nations regain authority, workers and communities gain meaningful governance, owners can transition without deception, wrongful dispossession can be repaired, and every consequential action remains contestable, correctable, and bounded by law.</p> <hr> <h1>Controlled cluster-adoption appendix — CEACA/0.1</h1> <p><strong>Patch version:</strong> The Hearthstone Protocol v2.0.1<br> <strong>Substantive base:</strong> The Hearthstone Protocol v2.0<br> <strong>Cluster release:</strong> <code>CEA-CLUSTER/0.1</code><br> <strong>Adoption specification:</strong> <code>CEACA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect of this appendix:</strong> none; it establishes compatibility, inheritance, routing, and fail-closed rules only.</p> <h2>A.1 Exact compatibility set</h2> <p>This patch participates only in the following controlled document set. A renamed body, later version, compatible-sounding policy, or partial implementation does not silently enter the release. The machine-readable cluster registry freezes the SHA-256 digest of every file.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Exact compatible file</th><th>Cluster role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td><code>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td>controlling authority interface</td></tr><tr><td>EIPHR/0.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-planetary-health-role-separation-matrix-v0.1.md</code></td><td>EIOI–Planetary Health role separation</td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.1.md</code></td><td>ecological observation, provenance, methods, uncertainty, interpretation, and advisory alerts</td></tr><tr><td>PHG v0.9.1</td><td><code>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.1.md</code></td><td>independent synthesis, participatory audit, cross-domain coherence, and strategic recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>HEARTHSTONE v2.0.1</td><td><code>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.1.md</code></td><td>voluntary conversion, claims support, title and tenure interfaces, trusts, rematriation, stewardship, and transition</td></tr><tr><td>WATER v5.2.2</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.2.md</code></td><td>watershed democracy, universal WASH, allocation, utilities, tariffs, guardianship interfaces, and emergency WASH</td></tr><tr><td>BIODIVERSITY v1.1.1</td><td><code>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.1.md</code></td><td>Indigenous-led stewardship support, restoration programmes, rights-review support, biodiversity finance interfaces, and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>OCEANS v2.3.1</td><td><code>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.3.1.md</code></td><td>marine stewardship, quotas and licensing interfaces, port-state measures, crew protection, precaution, and marine emergencies</td></tr><tr><td>SOIL v3.2.2</td><td><code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.2.md</code></td><td>living-soil protection, regenerative land-use programmes, microbial commons, tenure interfaces, certification, and remediation</td></tr><tr><td>MOS v2.8.2</td><td><code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.2.md</code></td><td>advisory ethical deliberation, rights imagination, plural translation, and structural self-correction</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>A.2 Controlling rules</h2> <ol><li><code>CERGTA/0.1</code> controls the separation of observation, interpretation, rights review, legal recognition, guardianship, affected Indigenous-nation authority, sacred and biocultural designation, claims, title, tenure, stewardship, allocation, permitting, inspection, urgent protection, restrictions, finance, adjudication, restoration, and closure.</li> <li><code>EIPHR/0.1</code> controls the division among EIOI, the Planetary Health Synthesis Office, and the Planetary Health Council. EIOI supplies evidence; PHSO compiles modular synthesis; PHC interprets and recommends. None may create a downstream legal or financial consequence.</li> <li>MOS is advisory only. Its Dynamic Rights Spectrum, philosophical language, developmental translations, structural-failure hypotheses, and guardianship metaphors never create legal status, classify persons, determine credibility, establish Indigenous consent, or activate consequential action.</li> <li>Hearthstone controls the cluster interface for claims, competing claims, title, tenure, trust constitution, voluntary conversion, rematriation, compensation, debt, tenants, workers, registry correction, receivership, succession, and dissolution. Sectoral ecological frameworks may provide evidence or programme support but cannot decide those questions by implication.</li> <li>Water, Biodiversity, Oceans, and Soil retain only the powers separately constituted in applicable law and recorded through the relevant CERGTA route. Framework membership, a council vote, a dashboard value, a target, a pilot, a future date, or a coalition size does not activate authority.</li> <li>Emergency identity and coordination use CDEE and ECRC. Ecological urgency does not create emergency command, and an emergency declaration does not create ecological title, guardianship, permanent restrictions, receivership, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, intelligence use, asset freezing, or analogous coercive execution requires separate SCPA authority. A commons or ecological claim may justify review; it does not authorize a raid.</li> <li>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable law, territorial and relational authority, consent process, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and whether to participate. Global or framework-created Indigenous bodies may support or audit but cannot consent or waive rights for another people.</li> <li>Essential minimums for water, sanitation, health, disability support, food, shelter, rescue, crew welfare, and ecological survival remain protected from commercial, tariff, certification, funding, listing, or transition leverage.</li> <li>Every handoff uses the canonical CERGTA states. Receipt, technical ingestion, silence, consultation, or partial use is not acceptance and never creates authority.</li> <li>Every consequential route keeps its own legal basis, decision-maker, affected persons, evidence, access controls, clock, expiry, challenge, correction, remedy, termination, and data-disposition record.</li> <li>Unknown, missing, stale, or incompatible versions fail closed for consequential action. Non-coercive care, emergency life-safety support, accessibility, explanation, and correction should continue wherever lawful and safe.</li></ol> <h2>A.3 Record inheritance and packaging</h2> <p>A local record may implement, extend, split, contain, import, or mark a CERGTA record not applicable. It may not erase the canonical identity, merge distinct powers, weaken an affected-person safeguard, or use a compound package to supply a missing legal basis.</p> <p>The forty-field CERGTA header, access classification, handoff state, independent clocks, correction propagation, remedy, termination, authority return, and data disposition are inherited wherever the substantive base creates or transports a consequential record. Advisory and observation-only records must state their prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>A.4 Registry and compatibility failure</h2> <p>The file <code>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> is the machine-readable release registry. It is evidence of the exact release set, not an authority record. A hash mismatch, missing component, unsupported local extension, or unresolved version conflict changes the compatibility state to <code>failed_closed</code>.</p> <p>A compatibility failure blocks new consequential cross-framework handoffs. It does not by itself terminate independently valid local law, erase existing rights, interrupt essential services, or authorize emergency or coercive action.</p> <h2>A.5 Closure</h2> <p>A cluster transaction closes only after all accepted handoffs, powers, services, persons, property, guardianship duties, claims, title or tenure effects, financial actions, emergency dependencies, coercive dependencies, corrections, remedies, clocks, credentials, records, and residual risks are resolved or assigned to a named lawful custodian without preserving expired power.</p> <h2>A.6 Editorial patch note</h2> <p>This patch is <strong>editorial only</strong>. No doctrine, power, boundary, record, gate, or test was added, removed, or weakened.</p> <p>Changes:</p> <ol><li><strong>§1 source freeze.</strong> Clarified as a provenance record rather than an operative dependency set, resolving an ambiguity against Appendix A.1.</li> <li><strong>A.1 compatibility set.</strong> Updated to name the current editions of Water, Soil, and the Moral Operating System, which were editorially revised after this release. The change is editorial in each case, so the correct response is to update the pin rather than treat the mismatch as a substantive conflict.</li></ol> <p>This document required no other change. Its record catalogue was already tabular, its crosswalk rows each carry distinct content, and its boundary statements vary meaningfully from row to row.</p>",1);function c(t){var e=a();r(454),n(t,e)}export{c as default};
